// REJECTED — a transparent type alias whose right-hand side names `Async[T]`
// (issue #1151). `Async[T]` is POSITION-RESTRICTED: it is legal only as the
// return of a function type, and an alias right-hand side is not that position,
// so the alias below has no legal reading at all.
//
// The refusal is anchored at the ALIAS DECLARATION, not at the arrow that uses
// it, and that is the whole point of this fixture. The reference erases aliases
// before it asks anything else about a type (`_resolve_type_aliases` runs one
// line ahead of `_validate_declared_types` in `check_and_lower`) and checks each
// right-hand side as it collects it. So `-> Later` never reaches the rule C1
// question that `t34_arrow_self_declared_async.rvl` pins: an alias of `Async[T]`
// cannot be declared in the first place, and the declaration is what is
// reported. The gate used to build its alias map from the same scan without
// asking, which left this program deciding on whatever the arrow below did.
//
// Legal spellings — both give the arrow a consumer obliged to await it:
//   * annotate the binding:  `let g: (Str) -> Async[Str] = v => suspending_op(v)`
//   * declare the parameter: `fn run(g: (Str) -> Async[Str]) -> Str { … }`
//
// expected error:
//   `Async[T]` is not a value type (`Async[Str]`)

type Later = Async[Str]

extern emission async fn suspending_op(v: Str) -> Str = @py { return v }

fn leak(x: Str) -> Str {
  let g = (v: Str): Later => suspending_op(v)
  return g(x)
}
